---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout title="Editorial method" description="Dating, citation, and linking conventions for the chronology.">
  <h1>Editorial method</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    This site is a chronology first: each article is anchored to a historical landmark, then linked
    by prerequisites and typed relations.
  </p>

  <h2>Dating</h2>
  <p>
    Every article carries a <strong>landmark</strong> with a display label, a numeric
    <code>sortKey</code> for ordering, and a precision flag:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>exact</code> — year (or finer) is well attested;</li>
    <li><code>approx</code> — circa dating;</li>
    <li><code>uncertain</code> — scholarly disagreement;</li>
    <li><code>bce</code> — BCE landmark (sortKey negative);</li>
    <li><code>unknown</code> — date unknown (<code>sortKey: null</code>), with a note.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Sources</h2>
  <p>
    Bibliographic references live in frontmatter and are cited in the body. Unverifiable claims
    stay in <span class="badge badge--draft">draft</span> status with explicit gap notes.
  </p>

  <h2>Relations</h2>
  <p>Typed links between articles:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>reading-prerequisite</code> — needed to follow the modern exposition;</li>
    <li><code>used-historically</code> — historically employed in the discovery or proof;</li>
    <li><code>generalizes</code> — later generalisation;</li>
    <li><code>alternate-proof</code> — another proof of the same result;</li>
    <li><code>application</code> — notable application.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Mathematics</h2>
  <p>
    Formulas use KaTeX at build time with <code>\(…\)</code> / <code>\[…\]</code> delimiters
    (and <code>$$…$$</code> for display). Invalid math fails the build and names the article.
    See <code>docs/math-subset.md</code> in the repository.
  </p>


  <h2>Quality standard</h2>
  <p>
    Article design, drafting, and review follow the project quality reference
    (criteria C0–C7): a successful article lets the intended reader understand a
    problem, acquire a new mathematical idea, see why it works, and recognise what
    it enables. Historical claims that are made must be accurate, sourced, and
    distinguished from pedagogical reconstructions.
  </p>
  <p>
    The authoritative document lives in the repository as
    <code>docs/quality-reference.md</code> (French, v1.0). Before an article is
    marked published, a completed validation sheet under <code>reviews/</code> must
    decide <strong>prêt à publier</strong> with no open blocking defects. Length,
    reference count, or section completeness alone do not prove quality.
  </p>

  <h2>Drafts</h2>
  <p>
    Drafts appear in local preview with a clear badge, but are excluded from production builds,
    Pagefind indexing, and the sitemap. Published pages never link to unpublished targets.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
